-- src/core/dst_invariants.ail
--
-- WI-A14 piece 1, under ADR-001 D7: the invariant set.
--
-- D7 lists TWELVE obligations and one more that is not per-execution:
--
--   "The runner applies reusable invariants to the returned outcome and
--    complete trace."
--
-- The twelve families below are each a runnable function over ONE execution —
-- outcome plus complete trace, never per step. D7's thirteenth statement ("the
-- discovery contract is itself an invariant") is over a PAIR of executions and
-- is `discovery_contract_findings` at the bottom, kept separate rather than
-- folded into a family, because an invariant that needs two runs cannot be
-- evaluated by a runner that has one.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- Every rule is its own constructor, and that is load-bearing
-- ============================================================================
--
-- Cluster 12 measured why: thirteen decode-mutation rows each asserting a
-- SPECIFIC refusal survived a corruption that would have made eleven of them
-- report a different rule, because each row asserts its own rule rather than a
-- non-empty finding list. A suite that asserts `length(findings) > 0` passes on
-- the wrong evidence and reads identically to one that does not. So: one
-- constructor per rule, `violation_rule` is total, and every acceptance row
-- names the rule it expects.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- THE PARITY FAMILY, AND THE ONE-LINE WRONG ANSWER (D6.4)
-- ============================================================================
--
-- D6.4 requires every LOGICAL `LedgerEvent` to reach the returned trace. At
-- HEAD before this item, 28 of 34 variants were classified Logical and 13
-- reached the trace, so 15 did not — `logical_variants_not_in_trace()`, which
-- WI-A8 exposed for exactly this invariant to consume.
--
-- A parity invariant written against that is RED, and there is a one-line way
-- to make it green that destroys the point: reclassify the fifteen as
-- DisplayOnly. That type-checks, the vocabulary still validates, every count
-- balances, and D6.4's obligation becomes vacuous — it blesses the exact gap it
-- exists to close. WI-A8 refused it deliberately and left the evidence in a
-- SEPARATE field (`reaches_trace_today`) so a classification could never be
-- confused with a measurement.
--
-- This module makes the wrong answer LOUD rather than merely discouraged, with
-- three checks that fail in different directions:
--
--   1. `display_only_baseline()` pins the six display-only variants BY NAME.
--      Reclassifying anything into that set turns `display-only-set-changed`
--      red. Reclassification is now a deliberate edit to a pinned artifact
--      under D8's compatibility rule, not a quiet field flip.
--   2. `d64_gap_register()` pins the variants that do not reach the trace, and
--      `parity_register_findings()` asserts the register EQUALS
--      `logical_variants_not_in_trace(event_vocabulary())` in both directions.
--      A variant that starts reaching the trace and is still registered is
--      `parity-gap-register-stale` — so the register can only SHRINK, and
--      closing a gap is forced to be recorded.
--   3. `parity_findings()` asserts, over a real returned trace, that every
--      record in it is a LOGICAL variant. That is the runtime half, and it is
--      the one D6.4 calls authoritative: "an external `ledger_emit` call is not
--      evidence that the event is in `LedgerTrace`".
--
-- WI-A14 CLOSED ONE OF THE FIFTEEN. See `d64_gap_register`.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- What the parity family CANNOT do today, stated rather than implied
-- ============================================================================
--
-- D6.4's obligation is parity between logical emissions and returned records.
-- `ledger_emit` is `-> () ! {IO, Trace}` and keeps no in-memory log, so there
-- is no per-run emission witness to compare the trace against, and this module
-- does not pretend otherwise: the runtime half compares the trace against the
-- VOCABULARY, and the emission-vs-trace half is discharged for the one class
-- D6.4 names as its explicit exception — streams — through
-- `ExecutionUnderTest.emissions`.
--
-- That channel is `ProviderExchange.emissions`. WI-C1 made it non-empty on a
-- live exchange — `live_ports` adopted `stepWithStreamRecorded` — so it is no
-- longer `[]` on every path. THE REASON THIS PARAGRAPH SURVIVES IS UNCHANGED:
-- the driver still has no consumer for the field (`session.ail`'s
-- `exchange.emissions` goes nowhere until WI-C3 builds D6.4's stream-parity
-- reader), and the deterministic adapters that could supply a replayable log
-- still report none. So a REAL RUN still cannot produce the executions this
-- check needs to select among. Per S8's complement, a check whose trajectory never
-- enters its branches certifies nothing and reads identically to one that does
-- — so `stream_parity_findings` is exercised by CONSTRUCTED executions in the
-- acceptance script (omission, duplication, reordering, content drift), not by
-- selecting among the executions a real run can produce. Cluster 12's limit
-- applies exactly: sweep-and-filter selects among things that exist and cannot
-- cover a space the producer does not reach.

